    "innovation": "This paper demonstrates a framework using existing climate model simulations to quantify the benefits of limiting global warming to 1.5\u00b0C versus 2\u00b0C, showing a perceptible reduction in the frequency of Australian extreme heat events at the lower warming level.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "record-hot summer of 2012-2013",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "The Australian continent experiences a variety of high-impact climate extremes that result in loss of life, and economic and environmental damage. Events similar to the record-hot summer of 2012\u20132013 and warm seas associated with bleaching of the Great Barrier Reef in 2016 would be substantially less likely, by about 25% in both cases, if warming is kept to lower levels.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "bleaching of the Great Barrier Reef in 2016",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Great Barrier Reef",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Events similar to the record-hot summer of 2012\u20132013 and warm seas associated with bleaching of the Great Barrier Reef in 2016 would be substantially less likely, by about 25% in both cases, if warming is kept to lower levels.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "heavy rains of December 2010",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "northeast Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The events we analyse cover a diverse range of climate extremes\u2014extreme heat over Australia (summer 2012\u20132013), marine heat (early 2016), large-scale heavy precipitation (December 2010), and severe drought (2006). These events had wide-ranging impacts, including deaths, severe ecosystem damage and high economic costs. [...] December rainfall in northeastern Australia, which was exceptionally high in 2010 (Fig. 1e) and associated with damaging floods, is similar across the four scenarios indicating a weak climate change influence in the model simulations (Fig. 1f).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "heavy rains of December 2010",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Queensland",
            "target_entity_type": "Country_Subdivision",
            "paragraph": "Precipitation extremes, such as the heavy rains of December 2010 in Queensland, are likely to see comparatively less change.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "severe drought of 2006",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "southeast Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The events we analyse cover a diverse range of climate extremes\u2014extreme heat over Australia (summer 2012\u20132013), marine heat (early 2016), large-scale heavy precipitation (December 2010), and severe drought (2006). [...] Precipitation deficits such as those of 2006 in southeast Australia, which were part of the longer Millennium Drought, would become slightly more frequent, but remain unusual (occurring less than 1-in-20 years on average).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": false,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Australian climate is strongly related to the El Ni\u00f1o\u2013Southern Oscillation (ENSO), which itself may change under future warming scenarios, although the nature of any such change is uncertain\u00b9\u2074.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "drought",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Australia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "In addition to the aforementioned climate extremes, Australia is also often affected by severe drought (Fig. 3).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "coral bleaching",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Great Barrier Reef",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A similar pattern of change can be seen in high Coral Sea temperatures in January\u2013March, such as those associated with the Great Barrier Reef coral bleaching event of 2016\u00b9\u00b3 (Fig. 1c).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
