            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Bushfires",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Wildfire",
            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Figure 4 | The changing likelihood of Australian extreme events. Examples of the likelihoods in a given year of similar events to four recent Australian extremes in a natural world, the current world, a 1.5 \u00b0C world and a 2 \u00b0C world. For the Australian drought case, changes in the likelihood of both precipitation deficits and high temperatures are considered due to their relevance. The best estimate is shown with the 5th\u201395th percentile confidence intervals in parentheses. Several of the impacts of each extreme event are highlighted. [Table entry for 'Angry summer 2012\u20132013' lists 'Associated impacts' as 'Severe heatwaves, Power blackouts, Bushfires']",
